# a layout is written for what is listed. One word of the cell says which fields the object has, in which order it got them and whether it has anything beside them: behind the tests stand loads and a literal,
# and the call as written stands in a block of its own, for an object of another layout, one that got its keys otherwise, a proxy, a text, null
# the list of the keys alone stays with the code in line of the built-in, which hands out a cell over a list that the run time keeps. Outside a loop a layout with an optional field has no arm
# the functions are handed to sink: nobody knows their callers, and the written type is only expected
# the two small types share no name: written as { x, y } and { x, y? } the front end gives them one layout, whose y is optional, and then neither has an arm
# f1 = values, f2 = entries, f3 = keys, f4 = loose, f5 = wide
# -- source: own-values-of-an-expected-layout-stand-behind-tests.ts
